Tu bas method call kar raha hai.

Ek real-life example

Suppose restaurant mein ja rahe ho.

WebClient

Tu waiter ko bolta hai:

Table number 5 pe jao → waiter ko bolo → ye order do → ye payment method use karo → order lekar aao.

Tu poora process bata raha hai.

HttpExchange

Tu waiter ko bas bolta hai:

"Pizza order kar do."

Waiter ko internally pata hai:

Kitchen kaha hai
Order kaise place karna hai
Order kaise lana hai

HttpExchange mein Spring waiter hai.

@PostExchange kya hai?

Ye annotation basically bol raha hai:

@PostExchange("/posts")

"Is method ko call karoge toh POST request /posts par jayegi."

Similarly:

@GetExchange("/posts/{id}")

means:

GET request.

@PutExchange(...)

means:

PUT request.

@DeleteExchange(...)

means:

DELETE request.

Header bhi bhejna ho?

Ye bhi interface mein define kar sakte ho:

public interface PostClient {


    @PostExchange("/posts")
    PostResponse createPost(
        @RequestHeader("Authorization") String token,
        @RequestBody PostRequest request
    );
}

Call:

postClient.createPost(
    "Bearer xyz123",
    postRequest
);

Spring internally conceptually ye banayega:

POST /posts


Authorization: Bearer xyz123


Body:
{
   ...
}
Toh HttpExchange ka main idea kya hai?

Ye line yaad kar:

WebClient = request ko programmatically likho.
HttpExchange = request ka interface/contract define karo, actual HTTP call Spring karega.

